+# Keep sessions in memory
+
+For whoever changes login or deploys the web app. Sessions now live in the web app's process because volunteers
+requested removal of the database access on each request to make the app faster.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres then supplied shared storage that survived deploys
+without adding another service. Volunteers have now requested a process-local dictionary instead to remove session
+database access. This gives up the previous guarantee that deploys log no one out.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: no code change; lost because a deploy still logs out everyone on the
+  restarted server.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost on the second force, a service the volunteers would run only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: the previous choice preserved sessions across processes and deploys, but every request accessed the
+  database; replaced at the volunteers' request.
+- **Process-local dictionary**: chosen to remove session database access without another service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions live in one dictionary owned by the web app process. This removes database calls from session reads and
+writes and adds no service to operate. No latency measurements are available to quantify the improvement.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The store needs no database connection. Operational limits and their workarounds are described in
+[session storage limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits).
